Just to clarify: Caster/castor/superfine sugar is the rather finely
ground stuff used in making meringues and pavlovas and such.  It's
maybe half the size of the normal table sugar, but not as fine as
confectioner's sugar (the stuff you call icing sugar).

You're saying the explosiveness falls at between those points in the
granularity scale?  I'm not objecting, just curious.

e.g. from coarsest to finest:

pearling sugar/sanding sugar (the decorative big glittery stuff)
coarse ground sugar (often used for sugar-in-the-raw packets that go
in coffee in the US)
Granulated sugar/sugar (the regular stuff)
caster sugar/superfine sugar (the stuff for meringues and such)
powdered sugar/confectioner's sugar/icing sugar (the stuff for icing)


